WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.854 --> 00:00:02.758
Week 6 at a grid operator.

2
00:00:03.469 --> 00:00:07.173
The pilot waits for system access, promised for week 5.

3
00:00:07.928 --> 00:00:09.484
Now IT says week 11.

4
00:00:10.484 --> 00:00:15.534
The RAG status: red, amber or green, with meanings agreed up front.

5
00:00:16.584 --> 00:00:20.647
Green: on track for the milestone, a dated checkpoint, without help.

6
00:00:21.425 --> 00:00:23.805
Amber: at risk, the team recovers it.

7
00:00:24.573 --> 00:00:27.813
Red: will miss, unless the steering group acts.

8
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.391
The team lead wants amber.

9
00:00:31.096 --> 00:00:34.382
But red is not failure: it is a request for a decision.

10
00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:39.324
The watermelon project: green outside, red inside.

11
00:00:40.048 --> 00:00:43.357
Green until two weeks before the deadline, then red.

12
00:00:44.478 --> 00:00:49.830
People hope to catch up, and fear for the messenger: the MUM effect, from keep mum,

13
00:00:49.923 --> 00:00:50.898
stay silent.

14
00:00:51.958 --> 00:00:57.066
By then the options are gone: bad news early is cheap, late is expensive.

15
00:00:58.093 --> 00:01:01.948
Report a forecast, where you will land: not 70 percent done,

16
00:01:02.087 --> 00:01:06.673
but savings of 180,000 euro, not 300,000.

17
00:01:07.718 --> 00:01:08.902
Colours on gut feeling?

18
00:01:09.619 --> 00:01:11.302
Ask: what would make this red?

19
00:01:12.045 --> 00:01:14.495
If nobody knows, green means nothing.

20
00:01:15.812 --> 00:01:20.735
Setting in advance who decides what is governance: one page, signed by the sponsor.

21
00:01:21.878 --> 00:01:25.269
Decision rights for scope, budget, schedule and people.

22
00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:30.874
The project lead decides within 10 percent on budget and two weeks on schedule.

23
00:01:31.821 --> 00:01:36.627
Beyond that, the steering group; above 20 percent or on strategy, the board.

24
00:01:37.643 --> 00:01:40.940
Those margins are tolerances: management by exception.

25
00:01:41.943 --> 00:01:45.856
Escalate when a tolerance is crossed, or will be within two weeks.

26
00:01:46.543 --> 00:01:50.154
Four weeks late, and the team cannot fix it alone: red.

27
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:56.318
Owner first, then the sponsor: you call the team lead and your IT contact.

28
00:01:57.361 --> 00:01:58.568
Keep it green for now?

29
00:01:59.322 --> 00:02:01.133
No argument in the corridor.

30
00:02:01.855 --> 00:02:03.759
I get it, nobody likes red.

31
00:02:04.478 --> 00:02:07.985
But now the sponsor can still help; in six weeks, not.

32
00:02:09.065 --> 00:02:11.898
As an outsider you can say what insiders can’t.

33
00:02:12.682 --> 00:02:13.657
Do it with care.

34
00:02:14.961 --> 00:02:17.980
In the steering group, in four parts: what happened,

35
00:02:18.096 --> 00:02:21.684
what it means for the milestone, what I propose, what I need.

36
00:02:22.737 --> 00:02:23.817
Red on the pilot.

37
00:02:24.601 --> 00:02:29.384
Access is six weeks late, so the pilot moves from week 8 to week 12.

38
00:02:30.405 --> 00:02:34.166
Proposal: start on paper in week 8, with two planners.

39
00:02:34.926 --> 00:02:38.176
Needed: your OK, and your call to the IT manager.

40
00:02:39.189 --> 00:02:43.682
That call is an escalation: a problem taken to someone who can decide or help,

41
00:02:43.821 --> 00:02:44.761
with a proposal.

42
00:02:46.053 --> 00:02:49.072
The sponsor agrees and calls the same afternoon.

43
00:02:49.781 --> 00:02:53.879
Escalating is the system working; escalating too late is the failure.

44
00:02:54.946 --> 00:02:59.184
Agree an escalation path up front: team, project owner, sponsor,

45
00:02:59.335 --> 00:03:00.473
a deadline per level.

46
00:03:01.233 --> 00:03:02.707
No agreement by Friday?

47
00:03:03.436 --> 00:03:04.434
Then the sponsor.

48
00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:09.960
Escalate the decision, not the person, and tell those involved first.

49
00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:14.782
The decision log: date, decision, who, why, what was dropped.

50
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.448
Two minutes.

51
00:03:17.176 --> 00:03:19.045
Month four, who decided this?

52
00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:21.737
Open the log, not the discussion.

53
00:03:23.068 --> 00:03:28.362
A plastics processor: line 2 first, with 14 percent downtime, or line 5,

54
00:03:28.513 --> 00:03:29.883
with a keen team lead?

55
00:03:30.889 --> 00:03:34.999
Bring two or three real options and a recommendation, not a problem.

56
00:03:35.734 --> 00:03:40.053
Weighed on decision criteria: cost, time, risk, impact on people.

57
00:03:41.068 --> 00:03:46.246
A, line 2: about 90,000 euro a year, but a sceptical shift leader.

58
00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:51.314
B, line 5: about 40,000, quick and motivated.

59
00:03:52.032 --> 00:03:55.236
C: four weeks on line 5, then line 2.

60
00:03:56.234 --> 00:04:00.356
Advice: C, as success on line 5 convinces line 2.

61
00:04:01.077 --> 00:04:05.117
Default: no decision by Friday, and the team starts with C.

62
00:04:06.093 --> 00:04:11.062
So a manager decides in ten minutes; a vague question costs three meetings.

63
00:04:11.774 --> 00:04:13.922
He picks C, logged with the reason.

64
00:04:15.274 --> 00:04:17.527
A decision stuck, from fear or a boss?

65
00:04:18.228 --> 00:04:20.376
Ask: what would you need to see to decide?

66
00:04:21.134 --> 00:04:23.502
And: who else needs to feel good about it?

67
00:04:24.563 --> 00:04:25.503
Unsure yourself?

68
00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:28.724
Say so, and advise a small test.

69
00:04:29.765 --> 00:04:33.294
A two-way door is easy to undo, a one-way door is not.

70
00:04:34.023 --> 00:04:36.647
Take the first fast, as low as possible.

71
00:04:37.721 --> 00:04:41.656
The trap: decided in the steering group, reopened in the corridor.

72
00:04:42.373 --> 00:04:46.959
Experts differ on consensus; many choose Intel’s disagree and commit.

73
00:04:48.328 --> 00:04:51.544
Eleven members have pushed three decisions for two months:

74
00:04:51.707 --> 00:04:53.878
18 percent over on contractors,

75
00:04:54.098 --> 00:04:56.083
a second region, two planners.

76
00:04:56.796 --> 00:05:00.070
Fifteen slides, then: let’s take this offline.

77
00:05:01.082 --> 00:05:04.855
Tell the sponsor honestly: nobody knows who may decide what.

78
00:05:05.587 --> 00:05:07.677
So decision rights on one page,

79
00:05:07.828 --> 00:05:12.275
and six voting members; a steering group that decides has five to seven.

80
00:05:12.996 --> 00:05:17.791
Experts argue about size; big groups feel inclusive, but rarely decide.

81
00:05:18.851 --> 00:05:22.241
Members who never decide often lack a mandate from their boss.

82
00:05:22.961 --> 00:05:26.932
Ask the two blockers first: can you decide, or must you go back?

83
00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:29.646
Help them, or invite who can.

84
00:05:30.734 --> 00:05:35.970
Only the three decisions on the agenda, with options and advice, three days ahead.

85
00:05:36.754 --> 00:05:37.520
No decision?

86
00:05:38.233 --> 00:05:39.371
Cancel and mail.

87
00:05:40.116 --> 00:05:42.891
Open with: today we need three decisions.

88
00:05:43.907 --> 00:05:46.914
The minutes: a decision log with owners and dates.

89
00:05:47.621 --> 00:05:49.978
A RACI chart helps only if read.

90
00:05:50.685 --> 00:05:55.015
Signs it works: short meetings, decisions in the first half hour.
